rehead 2017's the endless follows two  brothers who escaped a ufo cult when  they were younger but are lured back by  a strange videotape once they arrive  freaky things start happening and they  start to get the feeling that there's  something evil hiding in the darkness by  the time the credits roll the endless  has posed a lot of complicated questions  what's up with the entity and the time  loops what's with those freaky statues  and why is there a crossover with benson  and morehead's earlier movie resolution  well the invisible demon haunting our  heroes is a stand-in for horror movie  audiences who like their stories to have  dark endings and as it turns out the  creature drew the brothers back to the  cult in hopes of trapping them in a time  loop this particular desert is crowded  with different loops some which last  seconds and others which stretch on for  decades if you die in the loop or submit  yourself to the beast you'll be trapped  forever that's why the cult members look  so young they've been repeating the same  cycle every 10 years at the end of each  loop the entity tears them to shreds and  they start over again it's all part of  the creature's need for scary stories  with violent endings it turns out that  the guys from resolution are stuck in a  time loop of their own a revelation that  adds new depth to the 2012 film as for  those monuments they all represent the  evil entity they were constructed by the  victims stuck in the loops and they're  meant to express how these poor souls  viewed the demon judging by the apparent  age of some of these sculptures it seems  like this inter-dimensional creature has  been trapping people for a very long  time  christopher nolan's 2020 sci-fi flick  tenet just might be the filmmaker's most  confusing movie yet met with lukewarm  reception by critics tenet is a high  concept puzzle box that's more  interested in giving the viewer clues to  solve it rather than answers  hell happened here  it hasn't happened yet the protagonist  is a cia agent who finds a mysterious  artifact during a mission when the  artifact is stolen from him he's  recruited by a time traveling  organization called tenet to retrieve it  the protagonist and his handler neil  travel the globe to recover the artifact  but nothing and no one is as it seems  that sounds straightforward enough but  things are never so simple in a  christopher nolan movie and the ending  of tenet is one of his most confounding  the film's core narrative centers around  the concept of time inversion that means  the entropy of objects from the future  can move backwards so things from the  future can get brought into the past  those in the present move forward while  the future moves backward at least in  the present to achieve this effect of  two opposite movements at the same time  nolan shot the time inversion action  sequences twice one normally and the  other with the actors moving backwards  getting your head around the concept of  time inversion is the first key to  unlocking the rest of tenant most  viewers are bound to get lost their  first time around as the movie breaks  down linear time to render it a circle  that's why even the movie's biggest  defenders say it takes another trip  around to really get it to click  whether it's the sight of a skeletal  bear or a man turned into a fungus  annihilation is a movie that sticks in  your mind but even more compelling than  the gigantic shark gator is the film's  ambiguous ending after heading deep into  the shimmer an ever-growing  electromagnetic wave that mixes and  matches dna biology professor lena  encounters a metallic humanoid that  tries to copy her likeness using a  grenade lena kills the doppelganger and  burns down the shimmer but while she  saved the world that doesn't mean it's  smooth sailing for our hero lena's whole  reason for heading into the shimmer was  to discover what happened to her husband  kane he's a soldier who went mia after  heading into the world's freakiest light  show and then a year later he suddenly  appeared out of nowhere deathly ill  hoping to save his life and ease her  guilt lena accompanies four other women  into the shimmer to figure out exactly  what's going on what she learns is  haunting the cane back at the hospital  is almost certainly a doppelganger much  like the one that tried to take lena's  place however there's something odd  about the lena who finally escapes the  shimmer when she hugs her clone husband  we see the color in her eyes dance and  wave much like the lights in the shimmer  did the real lena make it back home or  was she replaced by the doppelganger  somewhere during their struggle or  perhaps the shimmer altered her dna and  turned her into something new whatever  happened lena is a new person forever  changed after literally confronting  herself  inspired by the french short film la  joute terry gilliam brings a fresh set  of eyes to an old-fashioned time travel  tale in 12 monkeys the killer cast and  gorgeous production design are enough to  get viewers to still return to this 1995  classic even if the ending is a bit  mind-boggling in a post-apocalyptic 2035  james cole is a prison inmate sent back  in time to stop the virus that destroyed  humanity he travels back to 1996 to  track down the source of the virus but  is continuously haunted by dreams of a  little boy seeing a man shot at an  airport in the end he finally catches up  with dr peters who's heading to an  airport to start the virus outbreak cole  tries to stop the doctor and instead is  shot by the police as it turns out his  dreams weren't dreams at all they were  memories of his younger self watching  his future self get killed the purpose  of cole's tragic life is just one of  many questions left at the end of 12  monkeys we never get to see if any of  cole's actions change the timeline but  it's heavily implied that his actions  were fruitless nothing will change the  past stays the same and the future is  unavoidable the whole affair is a bleak  take on time travel that appreciates  repeat viewings the movie never changes  every time you see it it seems different  because you are different stalker is a  classic but by today's standards it's  definitely a slow movie watching andre  tarkovsky's 1979 cerebral work of  post-apocalyptic sci-fi is a test of  patience but for those who have stuck  with it the reward has been great over  the years many people have watched this  movie and walked away with different  stabs at its meaning a deeply  interpretive film stalker is as  confounding as it is profound based on  arcadie and boris strogatzky's novella  roadside picnic three men the writer the  professor and the stalker go into the  radioactive zone which contains a  mythical wish granting room the trio  ventures through a dangerous land of  paranormal anomalies and unexplained  phenomena all while philosophically  sparring with each other and exploring  their motivations for the journey when  they finally reach the room however  there is an inexplicable anti-climax as  the three men sit in silence what  happens inside the room and the  significance of their journey is left  entirely ambiguous what exactly is the  zone and how has it changed the people  who dare enter it the final shots of the  movie show that the stalker's daughter  has now gained superhuman powers which  are implied to be a result of his  journey this question and others are  asked from the very beginning of stalker  and its lack of answers still sparks  vigorous discussion over 40 years later  if you're at all familiar with  pre-destination paradoxes you might have  some idea of where this ethan hawk  sci-fi thriller is headed  pre-destination is based off of robert a  heinlein's 1959 science fiction short  story all you zombies this 2014 sci-fi  puzzle stars hawk as agent doe a  disfigured time traveling agent who  moonlights as a barkeep after a failed  attempt to stop the bomber that wounded  him one day in 1970 agent doe meets a  man called john who reveals that he was  born an intersex female named jane and  had a child who was stolen by an unknown  man complications during birth led to  forced gender reassignment surgery so  jane began living as john agent doe  takes jon back in time to 1963 to get  revenge on the man who created this  situation only to realize that jon is  the person who got jane himself pregnant  so agent doe takes jane's baby further  back in time to 1945 and the child grows  up into jane this is the predestination  paradox and it only gets more  complicated as the movie continues by  the end it is revealed the movie is  about a single character the agent and  the bomber are also the same person as  jon jane and the baby and it's it's a  paradox right but it can't be  paradox after learning who he will  become the agent kills the bomber and  tries to close his own loop that's where  predestination ends leaving us to  imagine jon's endless nightmare and  wonder about the logic and existential  questions behind it all  robert pattinson fans might follow the  dreamy actor to the ends of the earth  but apparently not beyond our solar  system at least that's how the reaction  to 2018's high life would make it seem  while many critics found claire denise  erotic sci-fi drama mesmerizing and  thought-provoking a majority of the  audience seem to disagree this is likely  due to the slow pacing and cryptic  methods of storytelling that denis  utilizes monty is a criminal on death  row who's been placed on a ship full of  convicted killers heading towards a  black hole he and the others are subject  to experiments run by the mysterious dr  dibs who controls sexual activity on the  ship monty manages to father a child and  he works to keep himself and his baby  alive amidst the intense psychological  pressure of dr dibb's experiments high  life is focused on sex but many viewers  are still unsure about what exactly the  movie has to say about sexuality thanks  to its dreamlike quality its most  puzzling aspect is its representation of  the box a room that the prisoners use to  stimulate themselves denise described  herself as pro sex in an interview with  slate and clarified that she sees the  box as something positive however in an  interview with gq she also said that she  herself doesn't actually know what high  life is about saying i'm not interested  in understanding honestly it's not a  joke i don't need to understand i need  to feel good about something that's all  i need  although cameron crowe's 2001 sci-fi  adventure was a critical punchline in  its day vanilla sky has gone on to gain  a cult following this ambitious thriller  is an english language remake of  alejandro amenavars open your eyes a  trippy warning about the dangers of  technology and vanity narcissistic  playboy david ames is in a horrific car  accident after his jealous girlfriend  julie discovers his relationship with  another woman sophia after recovering  david rejoins society and starts dating  sophia again but his reality starts to  disintegrate in strange ways the ending  of vanilla sky reveals that the movie  has taken place inside david's lucid  dream that has been going on for 150  years after disfiguring his face and  losing sofia in reality david opted to  exist in a cryogenic hypersleep inside a  dream where he can live a fantasy life  until the technology needed to repair  his face is perfected his idealized  fantasy world gets ruined by a glitch in  the system so david is given the choice  to either stay in the dream or leap off  the roof to break the illusion and  return to reality  look at this  i'm frozen and you're dead  and i love you  what david ultimately chooses and what  it represents is unclear he decides to  jump and break free from his dream but  he doesn't know for certain if he will  wake up or what the world will even look  like now after so much time in the final  moments we hear a woman's voice called  to him but it's not clear whether he has  made it to reality or if he has finally  passed away  the bittersweet romantic drama eternal  sunshine of the spotless mind is a  collaboration between surrealism experts  charlie kaufman and michelle gondry when  joel and clementine break up they both  decide to undergo a procedure to have  their memories of each other erased the  majority of the film takes place in  joel's mind as he recalls his doomed  relationship with clementine as the  memories get deleted after going through  the procedure he retains one vague  recollection of clementine telling him  to meet her in montauk where they first  met despite the procedure's success both  lovers find each other again in montauk  and start again even after learning the  truth of their chaotic and painful  relationship this might seem to defeat  the point of the sci-fi elements or even  the story as a whole but it speaks to  the movie's central theme of love these  two have a special connection and  nothing can get in the way of that it's  a hopeful ending although seeing the way  clementine and joel's relationship plays  out makes one wonder if they're doomed  to repeat the same mistakes should these  two really be together or is the love  that was strong enough to bring them  back together also enough to break their  old dynamic according to vanity fair  kaufman originally conceived of a darker  ending in which joel walks away after  learning the truth knowing that gondry  and kaufman disagreed over whether joel  and clementine should end up together  further enhances the uncertainty of what  comes next for this eccentric couple  [Music]
 

